 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Structure",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "An LED on pins 2 and 3 drives a phototriac, which fires a power triac whose main terminals are pins 6 and 8 and whose gate is pin 5. The zero-cross types (AQH0213, AQH2213, AQH3213) add a zero-cross circuit (zero-cross voltage 50 V max); the random types (AQH0223, AQH2223, AQH3223) have none. Output is one normally open contact (1 Form A) for AC loads of 100 or 200 V rms."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Current ratings and derating",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "The 0.3 A parts carry full current up to 60 °C; the 0.9 A and 1.2 A parts up to 40 °C. Above that the allowed current falls in a straight line to 85 °C, where it is about 0.14 A, 0.42 A and 0.56 A."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input drive",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The input responds to current, not voltage: design for an LED current of 15 to 25 mA. If the input supply ripples, the current at its lowest point must still be within that range, and at its highest point must stay at or below 50 mA. Trigger current IFT is at most 10 mA."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input protection",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Reversed input polarity may damage the part permanently. Where reverse surges can reach the input, a diode placed in reverse across the input pins keeps the reverse voltage under the 6 V rating."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Gate and output pins",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Pin 5 is the gate of the power triac: no external part is needed on it, and it must never be joined directly to pin 6. Shorting any pins together while powered can break the internal IC."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output protection",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "The AQ-H has no internal snubber, so a snubber or varistor across pins 6 and 8 guards it against load-side noise and surges. For the snubber capacitor, metallized polyester film rated 250 to 400 V on a 100 V line, or 400 to 600 V on a 200 V line. The resistor limits the capacitor discharge at turn-on and damps ringing with the wiring inductance at turn-off: 10 to 100 Ω on a 100 V line, 20 to 100 Ω on a 200 V line; the general choice is 0.1 µF with 20 to 100 Ω."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unexpected turn-on and turn-off",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "A fast voltage rise across an off output can make it conduct with no input, even below VDRM. On loads whose current lags or leads the voltage, the triac may fail to turn off, and a zero-cross type may fail to turn on. Long load wiring adds inductance and spike voltage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Light loads",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Used below its specified load, the relay may misbehave, and its leakage current can make a small solenoid valve or AC relay load misbehave after turn-off; a dummy resistor in parallel with the load is the remedy for both."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inrush",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "Pick the part so the inrush peak of the load is no more than half the surge current ITSM. Tungsten and halogen lamps draw about 9 to 12 times their running current through a random type at worst (7 to 8 times with zero-cross). Motors start at 5 to 8 times running current, and switching one off puts about 1.3 times (induction) or 2 times (synchronous) the supply voltage across the relay. Capacitive loads and equipment with line filters can be tamed with a 200 to 500 µH inductor in series."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Over-current and over-voltage",
   "page": 15,
   "text": "The surge rating is for a few tens of events over the life of the part; a series reactor, current-limiting fuse or breaker in the load loop keeps a sustained overload from heating the junction past its rating. Against over-voltage, put a varistor or CR surge absorber over the relay or over the load supply."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Thermal layout",
   "page": 13,
   "text": "Keep the relay more than 10 mm from neighbouring parts that run hot, and allow for ventilation when several relays sit close together."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Mounting and handling",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "In the tube, pin 1 faces the stopper B end. Coating goes on after the board has cooled to room temperature. Condensation from sudden temperature changes in humid air degrades insulation."
  }
 ],
